/* HYPER-CHEMISTRY/catalog.js — generated by HYPER-CORE/tools/catalog.js; do not edit.
 * The titles of this discipline, for links and search in the other Hyper apps. */
Hyper.catalog("chemistry", [
  ["chemistry","Hyper Chemistry","Chemistry as a map of connected ideas: from atoms and bonds to equilibrium, acids, electrochemistry and organic molecules — every concept explained, every formula a calculator, every molecule something you can turn in yo",""],
  ["atoms-periodicity","Atoms and the Periodic Table","What atoms are made of, how their electrons are arranged, and why that arrangement repeats down the periodic table.",""],
  ["atomic-structure","Atomic structure","Elements and compounds, the particles inside the atom, and isotopes.","Atoms and the Periodic Table"],
  ["atomic-theory","Atoms, elements and compounds","All matter is built from atoms. An element contains one kind of atom; a compound contains two or more kinds joined in a fixed ratio; a mixture is just things mingled together.","Atoms and the Periodic Table",["physics:density","math:scientific-notation"]],
  ["subatomic-particles","Protons, neutrons and electrons","An atom is a tiny, dense nucleus of protons and neutrons with electrons around it. The number of protons fixes the element; the electrons decide how it reacts.","Atoms and the Periodic Table",["physics:electric-charge","physics:nuclear-structure","physics:nuclear-size","physics:bohr-model"]],
  ["isotopes","Isotopes and relative atomic mass","Isotopes are atoms of the same element with different numbers of neutrons. They react almost identically, but differ in mass — so an element's atomic mass is an average weighted by how common each isotope is.","Atoms and the Periodic Table",["math:percentages","physics:radiocarbon-dating"]],
  ["electrons-in-atoms","Electrons in atoms","Energy levels and spectra, orbitals and quantum numbers, and how electrons fill them.","Atoms and the Periodic Table"],
  ["atomic-spectra","Atomic spectra and energy levels","Heated or excited atoms give out light only at particular wavelengths, a fingerprint of the element. Each line is a photon carrying the energy difference between two allowed electron energy levels.","Atoms and the Periodic Table",["physics:photon","physics:em-spectrum","physics:bohr-model","physics:hydrogen-spectrum","physics:stellar-spectra","physics:lasers","electronics:leds"]],
  ["quantum-numbers","Quantum numbers and orbitals","Each electron in an atom is described by four quantum numbers: n for the shell and its energy, l for the shape of the orbital, mₗ for its orientation, and mₛ for the electron's spin.","Atoms and the Periodic Table",["physics:quantum-numbers","physics:hydrogen-atom-quantum","physics:electron-spin","physics:pauli-exclusion","physics:schrodinger-equation"]],
  ["orbital-shapes","The shapes of orbitals","s orbitals are spheres, p orbitals two lobes along an axis, d orbitals mostly four-leaf clovers. The shapes, their sizes and the signs of their lobes decide how atoms bond.","Atoms and the Periodic Table",["physics:wavefunction","physics:hydrogen-atom-quantum"]],
  ["electron-configuration","Electron configuration","The ground-state arrangement of an atom's electrons in its orbitals, built up one electron at a time: lowest energy first (aufbau), two per orbital with opposite spins (Pauli), singly before pairing (Hund).","Atoms and the Periodic Table",["physics:pauli-exclusion","physics:electron-spin"]],
  ["valence-electrons","Valence electrons and Lewis symbols","The valence electrons are those in the outermost shell, the ones an atom loses, gains or shares when it bonds. A Lewis symbol shows them as dots around the element symbol.","Atoms and the Periodic Table"],
  ["periodicity","Periodicity","The periodic table and the trends that run across and down it.","Atoms and the Periodic Table"],
  ["periodic-table","The periodic table","The elements in order of atomic number, in rows (periods) and columns (groups) so that elements with the same outer-electron arrangement — and so similar chemistry — fall in the same column.","Atoms and the Periodic Table",["physics:x-rays"]],
  ["effective-nuclear-charge","Effective nuclear charge and shielding","An outer electron does not feel the full charge of the nucleus: the inner electrons shield part of it. The net pull, Z_eff = Z − S, rises across a period and explains most periodic trends.","Atoms and the Periodic Table",["physics:coulombs-law"]],
  ["periodic-trends","Periodic trends","Across a period atoms get smaller, harder to ionise and more electronegative; down a group the reverse. Metallic character, reactivity and the acid–base nature of oxides follow the same map.","Atoms and the Periodic Table"],
  ["atomic-radius","Atomic and ionic radius","An atom has no sharp edge, so its radius is defined from the distances between bonded atoms. Atoms shrink across a period and grow down a group; cations are smaller and anions larger than their atoms.","Atoms and the Periodic Table"],
  ["ionization-energy","Ionisation energy and electron affinity","The ionisation energy is the energy needed to pull an electron off a gaseous atom; the electron affinity is the energy released when one is added. Both measure how tightly an atom holds electrons.","Atoms and the Periodic Table",["physics:photoelectric-effect"]],
  ["electronegativity","Electronegativity","Electronegativity measures how strongly an atom in a bond pulls the shared electrons towards itself. The difference between two atoms tells you whether their bond is non-polar, polar or ionic.","Atoms and the Periodic Table"],
  ["nuclear-chemistry","Nuclear chemistry","Unstable nuclei, the equations of decay, half-life, and fission and fusion.","Atoms and the Periodic Table"],
  ["nuclear-stability","Nuclear stability and decay modes","A nucleus is stable only with the right balance of neutrons and protons. Nuclides off the band of stability decay — by alpha, beta-minus, beta-plus or electron capture — towards it.","Atoms and the Periodic Table",["physics:strong-force","physics:binding-energy","physics:nuclear-structure","physics:radioactive-decay","physics:mass-energy"]],
  ["nuclear-equations","Nuclear equations","A nuclear equation shows a decay or a nuclear reaction. Mass numbers and atomic numbers must balance on both sides — but, unlike a chemical equation, the elements themselves change.","Atoms and the Periodic Table",["physics:q-value","physics:nuclear-reactions","physics:antimatter"]],
  ["radioactive-half-life","Radioactive decay and half-life","Radioactive nuclei decay at random, each with the same chance per second. The result is exponential decay: whatever the starting amount, half is left after one half-life, a quarter after two.","Atoms and the Periodic Table",["math:exponential-growth-decay","math:logarithms","physics:half-life","physics:activity","physics:radiocarbon-dating","physics:radiation-dose","math:exponential-distribution"]],
  ["fission-fusion","Fission and fusion","Splitting a very heavy nucleus (fission) or joining very light ones (fusion) moves towards the most tightly bound nuclei near iron, releasing millions of times more energy per atom than any chemical reaction.","Atoms and the Periodic Table",["physics:mass-energy","physics:fission","physics:fusion","physics:nuclear-reactors","physics:q-value"]],
  ["bonding","Bonding and Structure","Why atoms stick together, the shapes the resulting molecules take, and the weaker forces between molecules.",""],
  ["ionic-metallic","Ionic and metallic bonding","Ions held by their charges, the energy of a crystal lattice, and the electron sea of metals.","Bonding and Structure"],
  ["ionic-bonding","Ionic bonding","A metal hands one or more electrons to a non-metal, and the positive and negative ions that result hold each other by electrostatic attraction, packed in a crystal lattice.","Bonding and Structure",["physics:coulombs-law","physics:electric-potential-energy"]],
  ["lattice-energy","Lattice energy and the Born–Haber cycle","The energy needed to break one mole of an ionic solid into gaseous ions. It cannot be measured directly, so it is found from a Hess's-law cycle of measurable steps, or estimated from the charges and sizes of the ions.","Bonding and Structure",["physics:electric-potential-energy"]],
  ["metallic-bonding","Metallic bonding","In a metal, each atom releases its outer electrons into a shared \"sea\" that flows through a lattice of positive ions. The attraction between the ions and the mobile electrons holds the metal together and explains its con","Bonding and Structure",["physics:free-electron-model","physics:band-theory","physics:resistivity","physics:stress-strain","electronics:wire-sizing"]],
  ["covalent","Covalent bonding","Shared electron pairs, Lewis structures and the rules for drawing them, and polar bonds.","Bonding and Structure"],
  ["covalent-bonds","Covalent bonds","Two atoms share a pair of electrons, and the attraction of both nuclei to that shared pair holds them together. Sharing one, two or three pairs gives single, double and triple bonds.","Bonding and Structure",["physics:electric-potential-energy","physics:pauli-exclusion","physics:photon"]],
  ["lewis-structures","Lewis structures","A diagram of a molecule that shows every valence electron: shared pairs as bond lines and unshared pairs as dots. A short recipe produces it for most molecules.","Bonding and Structure"],
  ["formal-charge","Formal charge","A bookkeeping charge given to each atom of a Lewis structure by splitting every bond evenly. It picks the best of several possible structures: the one with the smallest formal charges, and negative ones on electronegativ","Bonding and Structure"],
  ["resonance","Resonance","When one Lewis structure cannot place the electrons correctly, the molecule is described by several structures that differ only in where electrons sit. The real molecule is a single blend of them, with averaged bonds and","Bonding and Structure",["physics:particle-in-a-box"]],
  ["bond-polarity","Bond polarity and dipoles","When two different atoms share electrons, the more electronegative one pulls the pair towards itself. The bond gets partial charges δ+ and δ− and an electric dipole moment — a continuum from purely covalent to ionic.","Bonding and Structure",["physics:electric-field","physics:dielectrics"]],
  ["bond-order-length","Bond order, length and strength","The number of electron pairs shared between two atoms. Higher bond order means a shorter, stronger and stiffer bond — though a double bond is not simply twice a single one.","Bonding and Structure",["physics:hookes-law","physics:simple-harmonic-motion"]],
  ["molecular-shape","Molecular shape and orbitals","VSEPR shapes, polarity of whole molecules, hybrid orbitals, sigma and pi bonds, and molecular orbitals.","Bonding and Structure"],
  ["vsepr","VSEPR: the shapes of molecules","The electron pairs around a central atom repel one another and spread as far apart as they can. Counting bonded atoms and lone pairs predicts the shape of most small molecules.","Bonding and Structure",["math:angles","physics:coulombs-law"]],
  ["molecular-polarity","Molecular polarity","A molecule is polar when the vector sum of its bond dipoles (and lone pairs) does not cancel. Polar bonds are necessary, but the shape decides.","Bonding and Structure",["math:vector-addition","physics:dielectrics"]],
  ["hybridization","Hybridisation","Mixing an atom's s and p orbitals into equivalent hybrid orbitals (sp³, sp², sp) that point along its bonds. It reconciles the shapes of atomic orbitals with the observed shapes of molecules.","Bonding and Structure",["math:dot-product"]],
  ["sigma-pi-bonds","Sigma and pi bonds","A σ bond comes from head-on overlap along the axis between two nuclei; a π bond from side-on overlap of parallel p orbitals above and below it. Single bonds are σ, double bonds σ + π, triple bonds σ + 2π.","Bonding and Structure"],
  ["molecular-orbitals","Molecular orbital theory","Atomic orbitals combine into orbitals spread over the whole molecule: bonding ones below the atomic levels and antibonding ones above. Filling them with electrons gives the bond order, and explains why O₂ is magnetic and","Bonding and Structure",["physics:wavefunction","physics:pauli-exclusion","physics:band-theory","physics:electron-spin","physics:magnetic-materials"]],
  ["intermolecular","Intermolecular forces","The weak attractions between molecules and the properties they control.","Bonding and Structure"],
  ["intermolecular-forces","Intermolecular forces","The attractions between molecules — dispersion forces, dipole–dipole forces, ion–dipole forces and hydrogen bonds. They are tens to thousands of times weaker than covalent bonds, yet they decide whether a substance is a ","Bonding and Structure",["physics:coulombs-law","physics:electric-potential-energy"]],
  ["hydrogen-bonding","Hydrogen bonding","A strong, directional attraction between a hydrogen atom bonded to N, O or F and a lone pair on another N, O or F atom. It explains why water boils at 100 °C, why ice floats, and how DNA and proteins hold their shapes.","Bonding and Structure",["physics:density","physics:latent-heat"]],
  ["imf-properties","How intermolecular forces set properties","Stronger attractions between molecules mean higher melting and boiling points, lower vapour pressure, higher surface tension and viscosity. The same forces decide what dissolves in what.","Bonding and Structure",["physics:latent-heat","physics:surface-tension","physics:viscosity"]],
  ["coordination","Coordination chemistry","Metal ions surrounded by ligands, and why their compounds are coloured.","Bonding and Structure"],
  ["coordination-compounds","Coordination compounds and ligands","A metal ion bonded to a set of molecules or ions (ligands) that each donate a lone pair. The complex has a definite shape, charge and coordination number, and multidentate ligands such as EDTA hold on especially tightly.","Bonding and Structure"],
  ["crystal-field-theory","Crystal field theory and colour","Ligands approaching a metal ion raise some of its d orbitals more than others. The energy gap Δ decides which light the complex absorbs — and so its colour — and whether its electrons pair up, which sets its magnetism.","Bonding and Structure",["physics:photon","physics:color-mixing","physics:em-spectrum","physics:magnetic-materials"]],
  ["stoichiometry","Reactions and Stoichiometry","Counting atoms by weighing them: the mole, chemical equations, and how much of each substance reacts and forms.",""],
  ["moles","The mole","Counting particles, molar mass, and what a formula tells you about composition.","Reactions and Stoichiometry"],
  ["mole-concept","The mole and the Avogadro constant","Atoms are far too small to count one by one, so chemists count them in moles: packs of exactly 6.022 × 10²³ particles, a number chosen so that a mole weighs the formula mass in grams.","Reactions and Stoichiometry",["math:scientific-notation","physics:kinetic-theory-gases"]],
  ["molar-mass","Molar mass","The mass of one mole of a substance, in grams per mole: the sum of the atomic masses in its formula. Dividing a mass on the balance by it gives the amount in moles.","Reactions and Stoichiometry"],
  ["percent-composition","Percentage composition","What fraction of a compound's mass each element provides: the atoms of the element times its atomic mass, divided by the molar mass.","Reactions and Stoichiometry",["math:percentages"]],
  ["empirical-formula","Empirical and molecular formulas","The empirical formula is the simplest whole-number ratio of atoms, worked out from mass percentages or combustion analysis; the molar mass then says how many of those units make one molecule.","Reactions and Stoichiometry",["math:fractions-ratios"]],
  ["reactions","Chemical reactions","Writing and balancing equations, the kinds of reaction, and the amounts that react.","Reactions and Stoichiometry"],
  ["chemical-equations","Chemical equations and balancing","A chemical equation lists what reacts and what forms, with coefficients chosen so that every atom — and every charge — on the left appears again on the right.","Reactions and Stoichiometry",["math:systems-of-equations","math:gaussian-elimination"]],
  ["reaction-types","Types of chemical reaction","Most reactions fall into a few families — combination, decomposition, displacement, exchange of partners, combustion — and, cutting across them, reactions that move electrons (redox) or protons (acid–base).","Reactions and Stoichiometry"],
  ["reaction-stoichiometry","Reacting masses","How much product a given amount of reactant can make, or how much reactant a given product needs: convert to moles, use the ratio of the coefficients, convert back.","Reactions and Stoichiometry",["math:fractions-ratios"]],
  ["percent-yield","Theoretical and percentage yield","The theoretical yield is the most product the limiting reagent allows; what you actually collect is always less, and the ratio is the percentage yield. Atom economy asks how much of the reactants could ever end up in the","Reactions and Stoichiometry",["math:percentages"]],
  ["limiting-reagent","The limiting reagent","Reactants are rarely mixed in exactly the ratio of the equation. The one that runs out first stops the reaction and alone decides how much product forms; the others are left over.","Reactions and Stoichiometry",["math:fractions-ratios"]],
  ["solution-stoichiometry","Solution stoichiometry","Molarity, dilution and titration — reacting amounts measured by volume.","Reactions and Stoichiometry"],
  ["molarity","Concentration and molarity","Molarity is the amount of solute per litre of solution, c = n/V. It lets you measure out a number of particles with a pipette instead of a balance.","Reactions and Stoichiometry"],
  ["dilution","Dilution","Adding solvent spreads the same amount of solute through a larger volume, so the concentration falls in proportion: c₁V₁ = c₂V₂.","Reactions and Stoichiometry",["math:fractions-ratios","math:exponential-functions"]],
  ["titration-calculations","Titration calculations","A titration finds an unknown concentration by measuring the volume of a standard solution that reacts with it exactly; the balanced equation turns that volume into the amount of analyte.","Reactions and Stoichiometry"],
  ["states-of-matter","States of Matter and Solutions","Gases and their laws, liquids and solids and the changes between them, and what happens when one substance dissolves in another.",""],
  ["gases","Gases","Pressure, volume, temperature and amount, the ideal gas law, and the molecular picture behind it.","States of Matter and Solutions"],
  ["gas-laws","The gas laws","Squeeze a gas and its pressure rises; warm it and it expands or pushes harder. Boyle's, Charles's, the pressure law and Avogadro's law describe how pressure, volume, temperature and amount move together.","States of Matter and Solutions",["physics:pressure","physics:temperature","physics:ideal-gas-law","math:fractions-ratios","math:linear-functions"]],
  ["ideal-gas-law","The ideal gas law","PV = nRT: pressure times volume is proportional to the amount of gas and its absolute temperature, whatever the gas. It turns litres of gas into moles and back.","States of Matter and Solutions",["physics:ideal-gas-law"]],
  ["partial-pressures","Partial pressures and Dalton's law","In a mixture, each gas pushes on the walls as if it were there alone. The total pressure is the sum of these partial pressures, and each is the total times that gas's mole fraction.","States of Matter and Solutions",["physics:hydrostatic-pressure"]],
  ["kinetic-molecular-theory","Kinetic molecular theory","A gas is a swarm of tiny molecules in constant, random motion. Pressure is their collisions with the walls; temperature measures their average kinetic energy. From that picture all the gas laws follow.","States of Matter and Solutions",["physics:kinetic-energy","physics:kinetic-theory-gases","physics:maxwell-boltzmann","physics:equipartition","physics:mean-free-path"]],
  ["effusion-diffusion","Effusion and diffusion","Gases escape through tiny holes (effusion) and spread into one another (diffusion). Lighter molecules do both faster: the rate goes as one over the square root of the molar mass (Graham's law).","States of Matter and Solutions",["physics:mean-free-path","physics:maxwell-boltzmann","math:exponential-functions"]],
  ["real-gases","Real gases and the van der Waals equation","Real molecules take up room and attract one another. At high pressure and low temperature this makes gases deviate from PV = nRT; the compressibility factor measures by how much, and the van der Waals equation explains w","States of Matter and Solutions",["physics:ideal-gas-law","math:polynomials","math:newtons-method"]],
  ["liquids-solids","Liquids, solids and phase changes","Vapour pressure, phase diagrams, the Clausius–Clapeyron equation and crystal structures.","States of Matter and Solutions"],
  ["vapor-pressure","Vapour pressure and boiling","Molecules escape from a liquid's surface until the vapour above it pushes back hard enough that as many return as leave. That balance pressure is the vapour pressure; a liquid boils when it equals the pressure around it.","States of Matter and Solutions",["physics:latent-heat"]],
  ["phase-diagrams","Phase diagrams","A map of pressure against temperature showing whether a substance is solid, liquid or gas. The lines are where two phases coexist; they meet at the triple point, and the liquid–gas line ends at the critical point.","States of Matter and Solutions",["physics:latent-heat","physics:temperature-heat"]],
  ["clausius-clapeyron","The Clausius–Clapeyron equation","Vapour pressure rises exponentially with temperature. A plot of ln P against 1/T is a straight line whose slope is −ΔHvap/R, which lets you predict boiling points and measure enthalpies of vaporisation.","States of Matter and Solutions",["math:logarithms","physics:latent-heat","math:exponential-functions","math:equation-of-a-line"]],
  ["crystal-structures","Crystalline solids and unit cells","In a crystal the particles repeat in a regular three-dimensional pattern. The smallest repeating box, the unit cell, fixes how many atoms each has, how tightly they pack and the density of the solid.","States of Matter and Solutions",["physics:crystal-structure","physics:density","math:solid-geometry"]],
  ["surface-tension-viscosity","Surface tension and viscosity","Molecules at a liquid's surface are pulled inwards, so the surface behaves like a stretched skin; molecules that cling to one another make a liquid flow sluggishly. Both grow with the strength of the intermolecular force","States of Matter and Solutions",["physics:surface-tension","physics:viscosity"]],
  ["solutions","Solutions","Solubility, the laws of Henry and Raoult, and the colligative properties.","States of Matter and Solutions"],
  ["solubility","Solubility and the dissolving process","A solute dissolves when its particles are pulled apart and surrounded by solvent molecules. \"Like dissolves like\"; the heat taken in or given out, the entropy gained and the temperature decide how much will dissolve.","States of Matter and Solutions"],
  ["concentration-units","Concentration units: molality, mole fraction, ppm","Besides molarity, chemists measure concentration per kilogram of solvent (molality), as a share of all the particles (mole fraction), or as a share of the mass (per cent, ppm, ppb). Each suits a different job.","States of Matter and Solutions",["math:percentages","math:scientific-notation"]],
  ["henrys-law","Gas solubility and Henry's law","The amount of a gas that dissolves in a liquid is proportional to its partial pressure above the liquid, and falls as the liquid warms. It is why soda fizzes, fish suffocate in warm water and divers get the bends.","States of Matter and Solutions",["physics:pressure"]],
  ["raoults-law","Raoult's law and vapour pressure lowering","In a solution, each volatile component's vapour pressure is its pure vapour pressure times its mole fraction. A dissolved solid lowers the solvent's vapour pressure; in a mixture of two liquids the vapour is richer in th","States of Matter and Solutions"],
  ["colligative-properties","Boiling-point elevation and freezing-point depression","A dissolved solute makes a solvent boil at a higher temperature and freeze at a lower one, by amounts that depend only on how many solute particles there are — not on what they are.","States of Matter and Solutions"],
  ["osmotic-pressure","Osmotic pressure","Water flows through a membrane that stops solute particles, from the dilute side to the concentrated side. The pressure needed to stop that flow, the osmotic pressure, is Π = icRT — the ideal gas law for dissolved partic","States of Matter and Solutions",["physics:hydrostatic-pressure","physics:pressure"]],
  ["thermodynamics","Thermochemistry and Thermodynamics","The heat a reaction gives out or takes in, and the entropy and free energy that decide whether it goes at all.",""],
  ["thermochemistry","Thermochemistry","Enthalpy changes, measuring them with calorimeters, and adding them up with Hess's law.","Thermochemistry and Thermodynamics"],
  ["enthalpy","Enthalpy and heats of reaction","Reactions give out or take in heat. The enthalpy change ΔH is that heat measured at constant pressure: negative for exothermic reactions, positive for endothermic ones.","Thermochemistry and Thermodynamics",["physics:heat-internal-energy","physics:first-law-thermodynamics","physics:latent-heat"]],
  ["calorimetry","Calorimetry","Measuring heats of reaction by letting a reaction warm or cool a known mass of water, then working back from the temperature change.","Thermochemistry and Thermodynamics",["physics:specific-heat","physics:newtons-law-of-cooling"]],
  ["hess-law","Hess's law","The enthalpy change of a reaction is the same whatever route it takes, so reaction enthalpies can be added and subtracted like the steps of a staircase — even for reactions that cannot be measured.","Thermochemistry and Thermodynamics",["math:linear-equations"]],
  ["enthalpy-of-formation","Standard enthalpies of formation","The enthalpy change when one mole of a compound forms from its elements in their standard states. One table of these numbers gives the enthalpy change of any reaction: products minus reactants.","Thermochemistry and Thermodynamics"],
  ["bond-enthalpies","Bond enthalpies","Breaking a bond always costs energy and making one always releases it. Adding up the bonds broken and the bonds formed gives a quick estimate of a reaction's enthalpy change — and explains where the heat of a reaction co","Thermochemistry and Thermodynamics",["physics:photon"]],
  ["chemical-thermodynamics","Entropy and free energy","Entropy, Gibbs free energy and spontaneity, and the link to equilibrium.","Thermochemistry and Thermodynamics"],
  ["entropy","Entropy","Entropy counts the ways the particles and energy of a system can be arranged. Gases, mixtures and disordered solids have many; reactions that make gas or dissolve crystals usually increase it.","Thermochemistry and Thermodynamics",["physics:entropy","physics:second-law-thermodynamics","math:logarithms","physics:third-law","math:combinatorics"]],
  ["gibbs-energy","Gibbs free energy and spontaneity","ΔG = ΔH − TΔS combines the heat of a reaction with its entropy change into one number: a reaction at constant temperature and pressure can go by itself only if ΔG is negative, and −ΔG is the most useful work it can deliv","Thermochemistry and Thermodynamics",["physics:second-law-thermodynamics"]],
  ["gibbs-temperature","How temperature decides spontaneity","When ΔH and ΔS have the same sign, temperature decides: the reaction switches from spontaneous to non-spontaneous at T = ΔH/ΔS. This explains melting and boiling points, limestone kilns and the compromise temperature of ","Thermochemistry and Thermodynamics"],
  ["gibbs-equilibrium","Free energy and the equilibrium constant","ΔG° fixes the equilibrium constant: ΔG° = −RT ln K. Every 5.7 kJ/mol of ΔG° at room temperature is a factor of ten in K, and ΔG = ΔG° + RT ln Q tells a mixture which way to move.","Thermochemistry and Thermodynamics",["math:logarithms"]],
  ["kinetics","Chemical Kinetics","How fast reactions go: rate laws and their orders, how concentration changes with time, and why heat and catalysts speed things up.",""],
  ["rates","Reaction rates","Measuring rate, rate laws and order, and how concentration falls with time.","Chemical Kinetics"],
  ["reaction-rate","Reaction rate","How fast reactants are used up or products appear: the change in concentration per unit time, divided by the coefficient in the equation so that every species gives the same number.","Chemical Kinetics",["math:derivative","physics:speed-velocity"]],
  ["rate-laws","Rate laws and reaction order","A rate law says how the rate depends on concentrations: rate = k[A]^m[B]^n. The orders m and n must be measured — they are not the coefficients of the equation — and the rate constant k carries all the rest, including th","Chemical Kinetics",["math:exponents"]],
  ["initial-rates","The method of initial rates","Find the orders of a rate law by running a reaction several times, changing one starting concentration at a time, and comparing the rates at the very start.","Chemical Kinetics",["math:logarithms","math:linear-regression"]],
  ["integrated-rate-laws","Integrated rate laws","Integrating a rate law gives concentration as a function of time: a straight line for zero order, an exponential decay for first order, a hyperbola for second order. Plotting [A], ln[A] or 1/[A] against time shows which ","Chemical Kinetics",["math:differential-equations-intro","math:exponential-growth-decay","math:logarithms","math:separable-equations","physics:radioactive-decay"]],
  ["reaction-half-life","The half-life of a reaction","The time for half of a reactant to be used up. For first-order reactions it is a constant, ln 2/k, however much you start with; for zero and second order it depends on the starting concentration.","Chemical Kinetics",["math:exponential-growth-decay","physics:half-life"]],
  ["mechanisms","Mechanisms and catalysis","Collisions and activation energy, the Arrhenius equation, mechanisms, catalysts and enzymes.","Chemical Kinetics"],
  ["collision-theory","Collision theory","Molecules react only when they collide hard enough and the right way round. Only a tiny fraction of collisions carry the activation energy, and that fraction grows steeply with temperature.","Chemical Kinetics",["physics:maxwell-boltzmann","physics:mean-free-path"]],
  ["arrhenius-equation","Activation energy and the Arrhenius equation","Rate constants rise exponentially with temperature: k = A e^(−Ea/RT). A plot of ln k against 1/T is a straight line whose slope gives the activation energy.","Chemical Kinetics",["math:logarithms","math:exponential-functions","math:linear-regression"]],
  ["reaction-mechanisms","Reaction mechanisms","Most reactions happen as a sequence of simple elementary steps. The slowest step sets the rate, intermediates appear and vanish, and the mechanism must reproduce the measured rate law.","Chemical Kinetics"],
  ["catalysis","Catalysis","A catalyst speeds a reaction by offering a route with a lower activation energy, and is regenerated at the end. It speeds the forward and reverse reactions alike, so it changes how fast equilibrium is reached, not where ","Chemical Kinetics"],
  ["enzyme-kinetics","Enzyme kinetics","Enzymes are protein catalysts that bind their substrate before converting it. At low substrate the rate rises in proportion; at high substrate the enzyme is saturated and the rate levels off at Vmax — the Michaelis–Mente","Chemical Kinetics",["math:linear-functions"]],
  ["equilibrium","Chemical Equilibrium","Reactions that run both ways and settle: the equilibrium constant, how to calculate with it, and how an equilibrium answers a disturbance.",""],
  ["equilibrium-basics","The equilibrium constant","Dynamic equilibrium, K and Q, Kc and Kp, ICE tables and Le Chatelier's principle.","Chemical Equilibrium"],
  ["dynamic-equilibrium","Dynamic equilibrium","A reversible reaction in a closed vessel settles where the forward and reverse reactions run at the same rate. The composition stops changing, but the molecules never stop reacting.","Chemical Equilibrium",["math:exponential-growth-decay"]],
  ["equilibrium-constant","The equilibrium constant","At equilibrium, the product concentrations over the reactant concentrations, each raised to its coefficient, always give the same number at a given temperature: the equilibrium constant K.","Chemical Equilibrium",["math:exponents"]],
  ["reaction-quotient","The reaction quotient","Q has the same form as K but uses the concentrations present right now. Comparing Q with K tells you which way a mixture will react: forward if Q < K, backward if Q K.","Chemical Equilibrium",["math:logarithms"]],
  ["kp-kc","Kp and Kc","For gases the equilibrium constant can be written with partial pressures (Kp) or concentrations (Kc). They are linked by the ideal gas law: Kp = Kc(RT)^Δn.","Chemical Equilibrium",["physics:ideal-gas-law"]],
  ["ice-tables","Equilibrium calculations with ICE tables","To find an equilibrium composition, tabulate the Initial amounts, the Change (one unknown x times the coefficients) and the Equilibrium amounts, put them into K and solve for x — keeping the root that makes physical sens","Chemical Equilibrium",["math:quadratic-equations","math:newtons-method"]],
  ["le-chatelier","Le Chatelier's principle","Disturb an equilibrium — add or remove a substance, compress it, heat it — and it shifts in the direction that partly undoes the disturbance.","Chemical Equilibrium"],
  ["vant-hoff","Temperature and K: the van 't Hoff equation","An equilibrium constant changes with temperature according to the enthalpy of reaction: ln K falls on a straight line against 1/T with slope −ΔH°/R. Exothermic reactions lose K on heating, endothermic ones gain it.","Chemical Equilibrium",["math:logarithms","math:linear-regression"]],
  ["solubility-equilibria","Solubility equilibria","Sparingly soluble salts, the solubility product, common ions, precipitation and complex ions.","Chemical Equilibrium"],
  ["solubility-product","The solubility product","A saturated solution of a sparingly soluble salt is an equilibrium between the solid and its ions. The product of the ion concentrations, each raised to its coefficient, is a constant: the solubility product Ksp.","Chemical Equilibrium"],
  ["common-ion-effect","The common-ion effect","A salt dissolves less in a solution that already contains one of its ions. Ksp still holds, so the shared ion leaves room for less of the other.","Chemical Equilibrium"],
  ["precipitation","Predicting precipitation","Mix two solutions and a precipitate forms only if the ion product Q, worked out after the dilution of mixing, exceeds the solubility product. Precipitation then continues until Q has fallen to Ksp.","Chemical Equilibrium"],
  ["complex-ion-equilibria","Complex-ion equilibria","Metal ions bind ligands such as ammonia, cyanide or thiosulfate into complex ions. A large formation constant leaves almost no free metal ion, and that can dissolve an otherwise insoluble precipitate.","Chemical Equilibrium"],
  ["acids-bases","Acids and Bases","Proton donors and acceptors, the pH scale, weak acids and their constants, buffers, and titrations.",""],
  ["acid-base-theory","Acids, bases and pH","Definitions, water's self-ionisation, pH, and strong and weak acids and bases.","Acids and Bases"],
  ["acid-base-definitions","Arrhenius, Brønsted–Lowry and Lewis acids","Three ways of saying what an acid is: a source of hydrogen ions in water (Arrhenius), a proton donor (Brønsted–Lowry), or an electron-pair acceptor (Lewis). Each takes in more reactions than the one before.","Acids and Bases"],
  ["water-autoionization","The self-ionisation of water","Water hands protons to itself: 2H₂O ⇌ H₃O⁺ + OH⁻. The product of the two ion concentrations, Kw, is 1.0 × 10⁻¹⁴ at 25 °C and fixes the balance between acidity and basicity in every aqueous solution.","Acids and Bases",["physics:resistivity"]],
  ["ph-scale","The pH scale","pH is minus the base-10 logarithm of the hydrogen-ion concentration in mol/L. Each unit is a factor of ten: lower pH is more acidic, pH 7 is neutral at 25 °C, and pH + pOH = 14.","Acids and Bases",["math:logarithms","math:logarithmic-scales","electronics:sensor-interfacing","math:scientific-notation"]],
  ["strong-acids-bases","Strong acids and bases","Strong acids and bases ionise completely in water, so the pH follows directly from the concentration. Only in very dilute solutions do water's own ions have to be counted too.","Acids and Bases",["math:quadratic-equations"]],
  ["weak-acids","Weak acids and Ka","A weak acid ionises only partly; its acid constant Ka (or pKa = −log Ka) says how far. From Ka and the concentration follow the pH and the percentage ionised, which grows as the acid is diluted.","Acids and Bases",["math:quadratic-equations"]],
  ["weak-bases","Weak bases and Kb","A weak base takes a proton from water only partly, leaving some hydroxide behind. Its constant Kb works just like Ka, and for a conjugate pair Ka × Kb = Kw.","Acids and Bases"],
  ["polyprotic-acids","Polyprotic acids","Acids with two or three protons give them up one at a time, each step with its own, much smaller, constant. The first step sets the pH, the amphiprotic ions in between sit at the average of two pKa values, and the pH dec","Acids and Bases",["math:logarithmic-scales"]],
  ["salt-hydrolysis","Acidic and basic salts","A salt solution is neutral only if both of its ions are spectators. The anion of a weak acid makes a solution basic, the cation of a weak base makes it acidic, and small highly charged metal ions are acids in their own r","Acids and Bases"],
  ["buffers-titrations","Buffers and titrations","Solutions that resist pH change, and following a neutralisation with a pH meter or an indicator.","Acids and Bases"],
  ["buffers","Buffers","A buffer is a weak acid and its conjugate base together, in comparable amounts. Added acid or alkali is swapped for the weak partner, so the pH hardly moves — until one partner runs out.","Acids and Bases"],
  ["henderson-hasselbalch","The Henderson–Hasselbalch equation","pH = pKa + log([A⁻]/[HA]): the acid constant rewritten in logarithms. It gives the pH of a buffer from the ratio of its partners, and tells you how much of any weak acid is ionised at a given pH.","Acids and Bases",["math:logarithms","math:logarithmic-scales"]],
  ["titration-curves","Titration curves","A titration curve plots pH against the volume of titrant added. Its shape — the starting pH, the flat buffer region, the jump at equivalence — reveals the kind of acid or base, its pKa, and which indicator will show the ","Acids and Bases",["math:derivative","math:higher-derivatives"]],
  ["indicators","Acid–base indicators","An indicator is a weak acid whose acid and base forms have different colours. Its colour changes over about two pH units around its pK, which decides where on the scale — and in which titration — it is useful.","Acids and Bases",["physics:color-vision"]],
  ["electrochemistry","Electrochemistry","Electrons moving between substances: oxidation and reduction, cells that make electricity, and electricity that drives reactions.",""],
  ["redox","Oxidation and reduction","Oxidation numbers, what is oxidised and what reduced, and balancing redox equations.","Electrochemistry"],
  ["oxidation-numbers","Oxidation numbers","A bookkeeping charge for every atom in a compound, found by pretending that each bond is ionic. When an atom's oxidation number rises it has been oxidised; when it falls it has been reduced.","Electrochemistry"],
  ["redox-reactions","Oxidation and reduction","Oxidation is the loss of electrons and reduction the gain. The two always happen together, because the electrons one substance gives up must go to another.","Electrochemistry"],
  ["balancing-redox","Balancing redox equations with half-reactions","Split the reaction into an oxidation and a reduction, balance each for atoms and charge using water, H⁺ (or OH⁻) and electrons, then scale the halves so that the electrons cancel.","Electrochemistry",["math:systems-of-equations"]],
  ["cells","Electrochemical cells","Galvanic cells, electrode potentials, the Nernst equation, batteries and corrosion.","Electrochemistry"],
  ["galvanic-cells","Galvanic cells","A redox reaction split into two half-cells joined by a wire and a salt bridge. The electrons must travel through the wire, so the reaction becomes a source of electric current.","Electrochemistry",["physics:electric-potential","electronics:voltage","physics:emf-internal-resistance","electronics:multimeter"]],
  ["electrode-potentials","Standard electrode potentials","How strongly each half-reaction takes electrons, measured in volts against the standard hydrogen electrode. The higher the reduction potential, the stronger the oxidising agent; any two half-reactions combine into a cell","Electrochemistry",["physics:electric-potential"]],
  ["cell-potential-gibbs","Cell potential, free energy and K","A cell's voltage is the free energy of its reaction per unit of charge: ΔG = −nFE. At standard conditions the same link ties E° to the equilibrium constant, so one measured voltage gives ΔG°, K and — from its temperature","Electrochemistry",["physics:electric-potential-energy","math:logarithms"]],
  ["nernst-equation","The Nernst equation","How a cell's voltage depends on concentrations: E = E° − (RT/nF) ln Q. At 25 °C every factor of ten in the reaction quotient shifts the potential by 59 mV divided by the number of electrons.","Electrochemistry",["math:logarithms","electronics:sensor-interfacing"]],
  ["concentration-cells","Concentration cells","Two half-cells of the same couple at different concentrations make a cell with E° = 0. Its voltage comes entirely from the concentration ratio, and the current flows so as to even the concentrations out.","Electrochemistry",["math:logarithms","electronics:sensors"]],
  ["batteries-fuel-cells","Batteries and fuel cells","Practical galvanic cells: primary batteries used once, rechargeable batteries whose reaction is driven backwards on charging, and fuel cells fed continuously with fuel and air. Voltage comes from the chemistry, capacity ","Electrochemistry",["electronics:batteries","electronics:supercapacitors","electronics:power-energy","physics:emf-internal-resistance"]],
  ["corrosion","Corrosion and its prevention","Metals returning to their ores through galvanic cells on their own surface. Iron needs water and oxygen to rust; salt, dissimilar metals and oxygen differences speed it up; coatings, sacrificial anodes and impressed curr","Electrochemistry"],
  ["electrolysis-topic","Electrolysis","Driving reactions with a current, and how much product a given charge makes.","Electrochemistry"],
  ["electrolysis","Electrolysis","Using a power supply to drive a redox reaction that would not go on its own: reduction at the negative cathode, oxidation at the positive anode. It makes aluminium, chlorine and hydrogen, refines copper and charges every","Electrochemistry",["physics:electric-power","electronics:power-supplies"]],
  ["faradays-laws","Faraday's laws of electrolysis","The amount of substance produced at an electrode is proportional to the charge passed: n = Q/(zF), with Q = It. One faraday, 96 485 C, is the charge of one mole of electrons.","Electrochemistry",["physics:electric-current","electronics:charge-and-current"]],
  ["electroplating","Electroplating and industrial electrolysis","Coating a part with metal by making it the cathode in a solution of that metal's ions. Current density and time set the thickness through Faraday's law; the bath, the current distribution and the cleaning set the quality","Electrochemistry",["physics:density"]],
  ["organic","Organic Chemistry","The chemistry of carbon compounds: how they are built and named, their isomers, the reactions that transform them, and the molecules of life.",""],
  ["organic-structure","Structure and naming","Carbon skeletons, functional groups, names, and isomers in two and three dimensions.","Organic Chemistry"],
  ["carbon-bonding","Carbon: chains, rings and hybrid orbitals","Carbon makes four strong bonds and bonds readily to itself, so it builds chains, branches and rings of any size. Whether each carbon is sp³, sp² or sp decides the shape of its corner of the molecule.","Organic Chemistry",["math:exponential-functions"]],
  ["hydrocarbons","Alkanes, alkenes and alkynes","Compounds of carbon and hydrogen only. Alkanes have single bonds and do little but burn; alkenes and alkynes have double and triple bonds that open up to add other atoms.","Organic Chemistry"],
  ["functional-groups","Functional groups","A functional group is the small reactive part of a molecule — an –OH, a C=O, an –NH₂ — that behaves much the same whatever carbon skeleton it sits on. Learn a dozen groups and you can predict the chemistry of millions of","Organic Chemistry"],
  ["nomenclature","Naming organic compounds","The IUPAC system gives every compound a name that can be turned back into exactly one structure: find the longest chain containing the main group, number it to give the lowest locants, and add the branches as prefixes in","Organic Chemistry"],
  ["structural-isomers","Structural isomers","Structural isomers have the same molecular formula with the atoms joined in a different order: a straight or branched chain, a group in another position, or a different functional group altogether. They are different com","Organic Chemistry"],
  ["stereoisomers","Stereoisomers and chirality","Stereoisomers have the same atoms joined in the same order but arranged differently in space: cis and trans about a double bond, or left- and right-handed mirror images about a chiral carbon. Living things tell them apar","Organic Chemistry",["physics:polarization","math:combinatorics"]],
  ["aromatic-compounds","Benzene and aromatic compounds","Benzene is a flat ring of six carbons whose six π electrons are shared around the whole ring. That delocalisation makes it unusually stable, so it keeps its ring and reacts by substitution rather than addition.","Organic Chemistry"],
  ["organic-reactions","Organic reactions","Substitution, elimination and addition, carbonyl chemistry, esters and polymers.","Organic Chemistry"],
  ["reaction-mechanisms-organic","Curly arrows: how organic reactions happen","Organic reactions are electrons on the move: a pair flows from where there are plenty — a lone pair, a π bond — to an atom that lacks them. Curly arrows track each pair, and the short-lived intermediates they create, car","Organic Chemistry"],
  ["nucleophilic-substitution","Nucleophilic substitution: SN1 and SN2","A nucleophile replaces a leaving group on a saturated carbon. SN2 does it in one step from the back, inverting the carbon, and is fastest on uncrowded carbons; SN1 goes through a flat carbocation, favoured by tertiary su","Organic Chemistry"],
  ["elimination","Elimination reactions","In an elimination a small molecule — HBr, H₂O — is removed from neighbouring carbons and a double bond forms. It competes with substitution; strong bases, crowded substrates and heat tip the balance towards elimination.","Organic Chemistry"],
  ["addition-alkenes","Addition to alkenes","The π bond of an alkene is a cloud of loosely held electrons that attracts electrophiles. Hydrogen, halogens, hydrogen halides and water add across it; with an unsymmetrical alkene the more stable carbocation decides whe","Organic Chemistry"],
  ["carbonyl-chemistry","Aldehydes and ketones","The C=O group is polar and flat: its carbon is an electrophile that nucleophiles attack from above or below the plane. Aldehydes are also easily oxidised, which is how Tollens' and Fehling's tests — and the old breathaly","Organic Chemistry",["physics:mass-spring-system"]],
  ["carboxylic-acids-esters","Carboxylic acids and esters","The carboxyl group, –COOH, is acidic because its anion spreads the negative charge over two oxygens. Acids and alcohols make esters — the smells of fruit, the fats in food, the polyester in clothes — and water or alkali ","Organic Chemistry"],
  ["polymers","Polymers","Polymers are giant chain molecules built from thousands of small monomers. Addition polymers grow by opening C=C bonds; condensation polymers join monomers with two reactive ends and release water. Chain length, branchin","Organic Chemistry",["physics:stress-strain"]],
  ["biomolecules","Molecules of life","Sugars, amino acids and proteins, fats, and nucleic acids.","Organic Chemistry"],
  ["carbohydrates","Carbohydrates","Sugars and their polymers, roughly Cₙ(H₂O)ₘ. Simple sugars close into rings and link by glycosidic bonds into sucrose, starch and cellulose: starch and glycogen store fuel, cellulose builds plants and is the most abundan","Organic Chemistry"],
  ["amino-acids-proteins","Amino acids and proteins","Amino acids carry an amine and a carboxylic acid on the same carbon; in water they are zwitterions whose charge depends on pH. Joined by peptide (amide) bonds into chains hundreds long, they fold into proteins — enzymes,","Organic Chemistry"],
  ["lipids","Lipids","Lipids are the molecules of life that dissolve in oil rather than water: fats and oils (triglycerides), the phospholipids of cell membranes, and steroids such as cholesterol. Long hydrocarbon tails make them compact ener","Organic Chemistry"],
  ["nucleic-acids","Nucleic acids","DNA and RNA are chains of nucleotides — a sugar, a phosphate and a base. Two DNA strands wind into a double helix held by hydrogen-bonded base pairs, A with T and G with C, so each strand is a template for the other: the","Organic Chemistry",["math:exponential-growth-decay","math:combinatorics"]],
  ["analytical","Analytical Chemistry","Finding out what is in a sample and how much: careful measurement, weighing, light absorption, spectroscopy and separation.",""],
  ["analysis","Methods of analysis","Uncertainty, gravimetry, the Beer–Lambert law, IR, NMR and mass spectrometry, and chromatography.","Analytical Chemistry"],
  ["measurement-uncertainty","Measurement, uncertainty and significant figures","Every measured number is an estimate. Reporting it properly means saying how far to trust it: significant figures, the difference between accuracy and precision, and how uncertainties combine in a calculation.","Analytical Chemistry",["math:scientific-notation","math:standard-deviation","math:error-propagation","math:normal-distribution"]],
  ["gravimetric-analysis","Gravimetric analysis","Measuring an element or ion by turning it into a pure compound of known formula — usually an insoluble precipitate — and weighing it.","Analytical Chemistry"],
  ["beer-lambert","Absorbance and the Beer–Lambert law","A solution absorbs light in proportion to how much absorbing substance the light passes: absorbance A = εlc. Measure the absorbance and you have the concentration.","Analytical Chemistry",["math:logarithms","physics:light-intensity","physics:em-spectrum","math:exponential-functions"]],
  ["ir-spectroscopy","Infrared spectroscopy","Chemical bonds vibrate like springs, each at its own frequency in the infrared. The frequencies a molecule absorbs reveal which bonds — and so which functional groups — it contains.","Analytical Chemistry",["physics:simple-harmonic-motion","physics:em-spectrum","physics:thermal-radiation"]],
  ["nmr-spectroscopy","NMR spectroscopy","In a strong magnetic field, hydrogen and carbon-13 nuclei absorb radio waves at frequencies that depend slightly on their chemical surroundings — revealing how many kinds of atom a molecule has, how many of each, and whi","Analytical Chemistry",["physics:magnetic-field","physics:electron-spin","math:binomial-theorem"]],
  ["mass-spectrometry","Mass spectrometry","A mass spectrometer turns molecules into ions, sorts them by mass-to-charge ratio and counts them. The pattern gives the molar mass, the isotopes present and clues to the structure from the fragments.","Analytical Chemistry",["physics:charged-particle-motion","math:binomial-theorem"]],
  ["chromatography","Chromatography","Separating a mixture by letting it be carried by a moving phase over a stationary one: components that cling more to the stationary phase travel more slowly, and so come apart.","Analytical Chemistry"]
]);
